      <h1 class="itbs-h1">Inverted Triangle Body Shape</h1>
      <p class="itbs-subtitle">Bust and shoulders noticeably broader than the hips, with a waist that's present but not sharply carved. Common among swimmers and naturally broad-shouldered frames. Here's the exact formula, a health-metric quirk worth knowing, and how to dress for it.</p>
    </header>
  </div>

  <div class="itbs-art">

    <div style="text-align:center;margin:0 0 8px;">
      <img src="/assets/images/Inverted-Triangle-Body-Shape-Formula-How-to-Dress-300x200.webp" alt="Inverted triangle body shape formula and how to dress" style="max-width:420px;width:100%;border-radius:16px;border:1px solid var(--itbs-border);" loading="lazy">
    </div>

    <section class="itbs-sec" id="itbs-what">
      <h2>What Is an Inverted Triangle Body Shape?</h2>
      <p>An inverted triangle body shape has a bust that's clearly wider than the hips, without the sharp waist taper you'd see in a top hourglass. It's the shape behind a strong, athletic-looking upper body with a narrower, straighter lower half. If you haven't measured yet, our full <a href="/">body type calculator</a> will tell you exactly where you land across all seven shapes.</p>
      <p>The formula: bust has to be at least 3.6 inches wider than the hips, and the gap between bust and waist has to stay under 9 inches. That second condition is what most people miss — an inverted triangle isn't waistless, it just doesn't have the dramatic 9-plus-inch taper that would push it into top hourglass territory instead.</p>
      <div class="itbs-note">
        <p><strong>Quick check:</strong> bust at least 3.6 inches wider than the hips, waist within 9 inches of the bust measurement. If your waist is more than 9 inches smaller than your bust, you're actually a top hourglass, not an inverted triangle — the styling shifts to favour showing off that waist instead of softening the shoulder line.</p>
      </div>
      <p>You can preview this silhouette in our <a href="/body-visualizer/">body simulator</a>: choose the female model, tap <strong>Inverted triangle</strong> under <em>Try a shape</em> and rotate the figure. Switch to the male model to see the same broad-shoulder pattern on a man.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="itbs-sec" id="itbs-common">
      <h2>Who Tends to Have This Shape?</h2>
      <p>It's especially common among competitive swimmers, and among anyone who trains the shoulders, lats and upper back heavily without matching lower-body volume — the upper-body width in this shape often comes from muscle as much as frame. It also occurs naturally in women with broader shoulders or a fuller bust and comparatively narrow hip bones, independent of training. Both routes land in the same measurement category.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="itbs-sec" id="itbs-compare">
      <h2>Inverted Triangle vs Top Hourglass — What's the Real Difference?</h2>
      <p>Both shapes have a bust wider than the hips, which is why they're constantly confused. The waist is what splits them.</p>
      <div class="itbs-tbl-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Trait</th><th scope="col">Inverted Triangle</th><th scope="col">Top Hourglass</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Bust vs. hips</td><td>3.6 in or more wider</td><td>1–10 in wider</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Waist definition</td><td>Under 9 in smaller than bust (soft)</td><td>9 in or more smaller than bust (sharp)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Visual read</td><td>Broad, straight line from shoulder to hip</td><td>Broad on top, but visibly nipped at the waist</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Styling focus</td><td>Add volume below, soften the shoulder line</td><td>Show the waist, balance is secondary</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>If generic "broad shoulders" styling advice has felt slightly off, it's usually because it was written for a top hourglass — someone with the same shoulder width as you, but a much more defined waist to work with. Our full <a href="/">body type calculator</a> tells you which of the two you actually are.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="itbs-sec" id="itbs-whr">
      <h2>The Waist-to-Hip Ratio Quirk for This Shape</h2>
      <p>Waist-to-hip ratio (WHR) can be misleading for an inverted triangle, and it's worth understanding why. Because hips are the smaller of your two key measurements in this shape, WHR — waist divided by hip — often comes out higher than it would for a pear or hourglass shape with the same waist size, sometimes landing at 0.85 or above simply because the hip number in the denominator is small.</p>
      <p>WHR was designed around variable fat distribution, not variable hip-bone width. A narrow-hipped, broad-shouldered frame can post a WHR that looks elevated on paper without carrying the same abdominal-fat pattern that actually drives risk in an apple shape. If your WHR reads high here, waist-to-height ratio (WHtR) is the more reliable second check, since it only uses your waist and height — no hip measurement involved at all.</p>
      <p>Run both on our <a href="/waist-to-hip-ratio-calculator/">waist to hip ratio calculator</a> and <a href="/waist-to-height-ratio-calculator/">waist to height ratio calculator</a> to see how the two compare for your own numbers. For the shoulder-to-waist ratio and other proportions specific to this build, use the <a href="/body-proportions-calculator/">body proportions calculator</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="itbs-sec" id="itbs-style">
      <h2>How to Dress for an Inverted Triangle Body Shape</h2>
      <p>The goal is the mirror image of pear styling: add visual volume to the lower half and soften the shoulder line, rather than reinforcing width up top.</p>

      <h3>Work and Formal</h3>
      <p>An A-line dress or skirt with minimal detail above the waist balances a broad upper body without effort. Wide-leg trousers do the same job in a more tailored direction. Skip structured, padded shoulders and boat necks — both widen the exact line you're trying to soften.</p>

      <h3>Casual and Everyday</h3>
      <p>Flared or wide-leg jeans and trousers, paired with a simple, unembellished fitted top, keep the frame in proportion. A deep V-neck or scoop neck reads better here than a crew neck or turtleneck, which sits flat across the widest part of the shoulders.</p>

      <h3>Special Occasions</h3>
      <p>An A-line gown with a soft V-neck is close to a default-good choice for this shape — clean through the shoulders, with movement and volume from the waist down. Avoid halter necklines and off-the-shoulder cuts, which draw the eye directly across the shoulder line and widen it further.</p>

      <h3>Swimwear</h3>
      <p>A bottom with ruffles, a bold print or a brighter colour than the top shifts visual weight downward. A scoop-neck or plunge top in a plain, darker colour keeps the upper half quiet. Avoid halter-strap tops and structured underwire styles with wide straps — they add exactly the width this shape doesn't need more of.</p>

      <h3>What to Avoid</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Boat necks, halter necks and any strap detail that extends the shoulder line further</li>
        <li>Structured or padded shoulders on jackets and blazers</li>
        <li>Skinny or tapered trousers under a loose or boxy top — the imbalance reads as top-heavy</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="itbs-sec" id="itbs-measure">
      <h2>Common Inverted Triangle Measurements by Size</h2>
      <p>The formula holds at every size — bust noticeably wider than hips, with a waist that stays fairly close to the bust:</p>
      <div class="itbs-tbl-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Frame</th><th scope="col">Bust</th><th scope="col">Waist</th><th scope="col">Hips</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Petite</td><td>36 in</td><td>32 in</td><td>32 in</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Average</td><td>40 in</td><td>35 in</td><td>36 in</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Plus-size</td><td>48 in</td><td>43 in</td><td>44 in</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>In every row, the bust-to-hip gap is around 4 inches — enough to clear the 3.6-inch threshold — while the waist stays within about 5 inches of the bust, well under the 9-inch mark that would push the result into top hourglass instead.</p>
      <p>Try the average row in the <a href="/body-visualizer/">Body Visualizer</a>: switch to inches, enter a 40 in bust, 35 in waist and 36 in hips, and the live analysis confirms the shape while you rotate the model.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="itbs-sec" id="itbs-faq">
      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <details class="itbs-faq"><summary>What is the exact formula for an inverted triangle body shape?</summary><div class="itbs-faq-body">
        <p>Bust at least 3.6 inches wider than the hips, with a bust-to-waist gap of under 9 inches. The first condition separates it from rectangle and pear; the second is what separates it from top hourglass, which has the same wider-bust pattern but a much more defined waist.</p>
      </div></details>

      <details class="itbs-faq"><summary>Inverted triangle vs top hourglass — which one am I?</summary><div class="itbs-faq-body">
        <p>Check the gap between your bust and your waist. Under 9 inches, you're an inverted triangle. Nine inches or more, you're a top hourglass. Both have a bust wider than the hips — waist definition is the whole difference.</p>
      </div></details>

      <details class="itbs-faq"><summary>Why does this shape show a high waist-to-hip ratio even with a normal waist?</summary><div class="itbs-faq-body">
        <p>Because WHR divides your waist by your hip measurement, a naturally narrow hip (which is part of what defines this shape) can push the ratio up even when your waist itself isn't especially large. WHR assumes fat distribution is the main variable; it doesn't account for frame width. Waist-to-height ratio, which skips the hip measurement entirely, is a more reliable second check if this applies to you.</p>
      </div></details>

      <details class="itbs-faq"><summary>Is inverted triangle common among athletes and swimmers?</summary><div class="itbs-faq-body">
        <p>Yes, particularly swimmers and anyone who trains the shoulders, lats and upper back heavily. Muscle development in that area widens the bust-to-hip gap without changing the hips at all, which can shift someone from rectangle toward inverted triangle over time. It also occurs naturally in people with broader shoulders or a fuller bust and narrower hip bones, with no training involved.</p>
      </div></details>

      <details class="itbs-faq"><summary>Does strength training make an inverted triangle shape more pronounced?</summary><div class="itbs-faq-body">
        <p>Upper-body training — shoulders, back, chest — can widen the visual bust-to-hip gap further. If the goal is more balance rather than more contrast, shifting training emphasis toward glutes and legs, alongside the same upper-body work, tends to even out the proportion over time without requiring less upper-body strength.</p>
      </div></details>

      <details class="itbs-faq"><summary>What's the most flattering swimsuit for an inverted triangle?</summary><div class="itbs-faq-body">
        <p>A patterned, ruffled or brighter-coloured bottom paired with a plain, darker scoop-neck or plunge top. The aim is the opposite of pear styling — shift visual weight down and keep the shoulder line as quiet as possible.</p>
      </div></details>
    </section>

    <p>Most inverted-triangle styling advice online focuses entirely on "hiding" broad shoulders, which misses the more useful framing: the shoulder line isn't a problem to disguise, it's a fixed proportion to balance — and balance, unlike disguise, actually works with tailoring instead of against it.</p>

